o this I  want to go a step further I don't think  it's crazy for couples to maintain  personal bank accounts and then a shared  bank account that  used for shared expenses you don't have  to contribute to the shared bank account  equally you might determine your monthly  expenses or $2,000 so you are each going  to contribute a different amount maybe  it's one person contributes $1,200  towards the 2000 and monthly spending  and the other person contributes $800  towards the monthly spending you know I  have a friends in two situations when it  comes to couple spending some where one  person basically pays for nothing right  and typically in in modern Western  American society when that's the case  you've got a man that is earning more in  a heterosexual relationship with a woman  who is earning less in the friends that  I know and you some kind of situations  where the woman who sometimes is my  friend will say yeah you know my  significant other pays for everything I  kind of feel bad I don't really pay for  anything and yeah he makes more than me  but I still should be should be paying  some someone and I think that that's  where the paying in proportion to your  income makes a lot of sense the other  situation I'm aware of is where people  split everything despite the fact that  there are very unequal incomes and that  I also think is not necessarily fair and  I think it also is not that great for  the couple in the sense that if you have  one person in a relationship that makes  a lot more money than the other the  access to for example vacations that  that couple might have if they shared  the expenses of the vacations in  proportion to their income they might be  able to do a lot more vacationing than  if they go in saying we're only going on  vacation we can both afford 50% of the  cost there definitely also seems to be a  difference when you talk about the  initial phases in a relationship say  you're on your first couple days or if  when you're courting the other person  right is when you're in a  well-established relationship and I  think that's when gender roles come in a  lot more to play because typically in  the United States I mean the man is just  expected to pick up the bill at the end  of a dinner or if you go out for drinks  or something like that  especially when it comes to the first  few dates now what's fascinating about  that is I talked to more and more women  who say oh I did not allow him to pay  for the first date because I'm not  trying to establish anything where  there's an expectation so even though  it's not that I'm on many women have  told me it's not that I'm uncomfortable  with someone taking me out but it's the  first date and I don't want to set the  tone that I would even remotely expect  to be played paid for and so I know more  and more women that will go out of their  way to either pay on the first day or at  least to split the bill on the first  date but yeah you're talking more in  this initial courtship phase yeah versus  in the sort of longer term relationship  mode out because I think that you know  the man in that instance may feel like  the state has gone so swimmingly and  things are going great but I can't ruin  it by not offering to pay the entirety  of the bill because just that's the  customs that we have in this country I  don't know about others and you know you  don't want to break that norm even  though that may be the right thing to do  now that is a sexist by its very  definition  norm right I mean you agree with that  Mary yes categories all right everybody  make a note Leila  that's my answer do you think I will get  hate for this probably it regardless of  how you answer this question you're  going to get hate because either you're  splitting things up in equitably or you  are taking a sexist approach I would say  okay fair enough you're going to get  hate all right well regardless I'm ready  for it bring it on folks

